<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shoghi Effendi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1896–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Shoghí Effendi, Shoghí Rabbání</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shoghi Effendi was the Guardian of the Baháʼí Faith from 1922 until his death in 1957, the grandson and successor of ʻAbdu'l-Bahá. Born Shoghí Rabbání in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1896 or 1897, he studied at the Syrian Protestant College and at Balliol College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and on returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> at the end of 1921 learned that ʻAbdu'l-Bahá's will named him Guardian. As the faith's authorised interpreter he translated the writings of its central figures, built its global administration, sent more than 17,500 letters and began work on a Baháʼí World Center in Haifa; Baháʼís worldwide grew from 100,000 to 400,000 under his leadership. He died of Asian flu during a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoghi-effendi.jpg" alt="Shoghi Effendi" width="330" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoghi_Effendi2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…faith places great weight on the Covenant: Bahá’u’lláh named his son ʻAbdu’l-Bahá as the interpreter of his writings, and the faith’s authority passed in turn to his great-grandson <b>Shoghi Effendi</b> and then to the Universal House of Justice, a succession that has preserved the unity of the community.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
